\label{prop:inst-ladder}
  Suppose that sequences $(\rho_k)$ in $\mathfrak{D}(H_1)$ and $(\sigma_l)$ in $\mathfrak{D}(H_2)$ give iterated limits $L_1\neq L_2$ of $\varphi_C(\rho_k,\sigma_l)$.
  Then for every $\delta<|L_1-L_2|$, there is an infinite $\delta$-ladder for $C$ whose states are taken from these sequences.

\label{thm:trees}
  If $C$ is unstable, then there is $\delta>0$ such that for every $n$, there is a $\delta$-tree of height $n$ for $C$;
  its leaves and nodes can be taken from any pair of sequences witnessing the instability, and in particular can be taken pure if those are pure.

\label{def:uniform}
  The observable $C$ is \emph{uniformly stable} if for every $\delta>0$, the lengths of $\delta$-ladders for $C$ are bounded.
  A bounded observable $C$ is \emph{uniformly stable} if its hermitian parts $\frac12(C+C^*)$ and $\frac1{2i}(C-C^*)$ are uniformly stable.

\label{prop:uniform-examples}
  Every self-adjoint $C\in B(H_1)\otimes_{\min}B(H_2)$ is uniformly stable, and so is every self-adjoint $C$ with $\varphi_C(\rho,\sigma)=\langle u(\rho),v(\sigma)\rangle$ for bounded maps $u$ and $v$ into a Hilbert space.
  In particular, the swap operator, the projections $P_\pm$ and the equality tests $[A=B]$ are uniformly stable.

\label{prop:littlestone}
  For every self-adjoint $C\in B(H_1\otimes H_2)$, every $\gamma$ and every $\delta>0$, we have $\operatorname{mist}_C(\gamma,\delta)=d_\gamma$.
  Consequently, the observable $C$ is uniformly stable if and only if $\operatorname{mist}_C(\delta)$ is finite for every $\delta>0$, and then $\operatorname{mist}_C(\delta)=d_C(\delta)$.

\label{cor:orthoposet}
  The stable projections form an orthomodular poset:
  if $P$ is stable, then so is $I-P$, and if $P$ and $Q$ are stable with $PQ=0$, then the projection $P+Q$ is stable.
